# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and restart the dev server.
#
# Nothing here is required. Without the Epic variables the app still runs: the
# connect panel says it is not configured and the explorer shows the
# de-identified sample record. Without POSTGRES_URL the versions API answers 503
# and the header reports that publishing is unavailable.

# --- Published versions -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Any Postgres database; the schema is created lazily on first use, so an empty
# database is fine. Point this at a POOLED endpoint (Neon's `-pooler` host,
# Supabase's pgBouncer port) — serverless functions open a connection per warm
# instance and will exhaust a direct connection limit. `DATABASE_URL` is
# accepted as an alias, which is what most providers inject by default.
#
# This replaces the Cloudflare D1 + R2 pair the app used before it moved to
# Vercel; the overlay now lives in a jsonb column rather than an R2 object.
POSTGRES_URL=postgres://user:password@host/db?sslmode=require

# --- Connecting a real MyChart record ---------------------------------------
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed because they are inlined into the client bundle at BUILD
# time, not read at runtime. A PKCE client id is public by design (there is no
# client secret in this flow), so this is safe — but it does mean a deployed
# build needs them set when `next build` runs, not added afterwards.

# There is no global "sandbox mode". Which client id is used follows the
# environment of the organization you search for in the app:
#
#   Any production organization  ->  NEXT_PUBLIC_EPIC_CLIENT_ID
#   Epic Sandbox                 ->  NEXT_PUBLIC_EPIC_SANDBOX_CLIENT_ID
#
# The picker defaults to UCSF, so to test against Epic's sandbox you must search
# for "Epic Sandbox" there. Setting only the sandbox id is fine: the production
# organizations are then shown as unconfigured.

# Every production organization. Epic registers your app once and each customer
# health system enables it, so one id covers all ~480 organizations in
# `public/directory/epic-r4.json`. Requires Epic app review and the health system
# to have enabled your app.
NEXT_PUBLIC_EPIC_CLIENT_ID=your-epic-production-client-id

# Epic's non-production id, issued immediately on registration. Works against
# the Epic Sandbox provider with Epic's test patients — start here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_EPIC_SANDBOX_CLIENT_ID=your-epic-non-production-client-id

# Overrides the default scope: openid fhirUser launch/patient patient/*.rs
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EPIC_SCOPE=

# Raises WebContainer's rate limits. Optional.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WEBCONTAINER_API_KEY=
